body:
Closes #982.

## Root cause and reproduction

Released Formal AI versions persisted an unversioned `demo_memory` document without a compatibility contract. Operators could not determine whether a candidate binary could safely read a long-lived volume, and ordinary parsing discarded unknown event fields on the next write. There was also no distinct migration transaction with the shared writer lock, a verified rollback copy, atomic commit, or durable receipt.

The regression suite reproduces those risks with schema-1 fixtures, a zero-byte released store, future and malformed schemas, a held writer lock, an interrupted pre-commit migration, and output produced by the actual released writer. The latter exposed an additional root cause during verification: released writers use C-style quoted-scalar escapes, while the canonical Links Notation grammar uses different escaping. Upgrade validation now accepts the exact released wire format rather than rejecting valid persisted JSON, quotes, newlines, and backslashes.

## Solution

- add side-effect-free `formal-ai memory upgrade-status --format json` preflight with explicit detected/minimum/maximum/target schema fields and structured refusal details;
- add explicit `formal-ai memory migrate` using the ordinary writer lock, a byte- and SHA-256-verified backup, marker-only transformation, same-directory staged writes, `fsync`, atomic rename, and a durable JSON receipt;
- preserve source schema during ordinary reads/writes, preserve unknown event metadata, and refuse writes to incompatible future/corrupt stores;
- expose persisted-memory compatibility through `/health` without triggering migration;
- document rollout, idempotent retry, rollback, and compatibility guarantees;
- exercise the released `0.335.0` container and candidate image against one named volume through write, preflight, migration, load/query/export, rollback, and released-image reopen.

## Verification

- `cargo test --verbose -- --test-threads=4`: all unaffected targets passed (2,602 unit tests plus integration binaries); the sole failure was stale generated self-AST metadata after the final parser edit, and the exact drift test passes after regenerating/committing that metadata;
- issue #982 integration suite: 13/13 passed, including lossless migration, interruption/retry, lock refusal, malformed/future refusal, zero-byte stores, released-writer escapes, schema retention, health, and rollback;
- exact released-to-candidate Docker lifecycle passed locally using the `v0.335.0` source binary packaged in the released container layout;
- `cargo fmt -- --check`;
- `cargo check --all-targets --all-features`;
- `cargo clippy --lib --bins --tests --all-features -- -D warnings`;
- `cargo check --examples --all-features`;
- doctests and Rustdoc with warnings denied, including the docs.rs no-default-features profile;
- repository file-size, hardcoded-language, secret, self-AST, and self-hosting checks.

## Rollback

The receipt records the byte-exact backup path and both SHA-256 digests. Stop writers, restore the recorded backup over the memory path, and restart the prior binary. The Docker lifecycle verifies the restored bytes and confirms that `0.335.0` reopens the rolled-back volume. Schema 2 is additive, so the prior reader can also inspect the migrated file before rollback.

## Visual evidence

Not applicable: this change has no UI or layout changes.
